Guessing Game: Learning to Search Through Questions
Where the idea came from

The idea for this project started from a fairly random observation.

I had been self-learning Reinforcement Learning and thinking about how to connect some of the concepts I was studying to something more intuitive. Around the same time, I had been seeing YouTubers play the recent Guess Who? game where one person thinks of a character and everyone else has to figure out who it is by asking questions.

What interested me was the way people actually played.

Someone asks a question, immediately eliminates a group of possible characters, and moves on. Sometimes they make a surprisingly early guess. Sometimes they ask a question that turns out not to help very much. Sometimes they seem to be following a strategy, and sometimes they appear to be guessing almost randomly.

That made me wonder:

What would it look like to turn this process into an environment for an agent?

Could an agent learn which questions are useful? How would it reduce a large pool of possibilities? When should it continue searching, and when should it guess?

I did not start this project with a complete RL environment in mind. Initially, I simply wanted to build the game and experiment with how an algorithm could play it.

That simple idea eventually grew into something much larger.

The Game

The game is an automated, high-cardinality version of Guess Who?

Instead of a small board of characters, the environment works with a dataset containing thousands of candidates. Each character has a collection of attributes such as:

Region
Industry
Age
Hair color
Sex

One character is selected as the hidden target.

The agent's job is to identify that character by asking binary questions:

Is Region = 'Europe'?

The opponent responds:

Yes

or:

No

The search space is then updated based on that answer.

For example:

Initial candidates: 1,000


Is Region = 'Europe'?
Yes


Remaining candidates: 158


Is Age = 'senior'?
Yes


Remaining candidates: 18


Is Hair color = 'blonde'?
No


Remaining candidates: 11

The process continues until the agent identifies the target or reaches the episode's step limit.

At its core, the problem is a sequential search task:

Candidates→Question→Answer→Updated search space→Next decision

The interesting part is deciding what that next decision should be.

From a Game to an Experiment

My first approach was not Reinforcement Learning.

I began by experimenting with an entropy-based agent. The basic idea was that a feature with a more diverse distribution might contain more information and therefore be a better feature to ask about.

As I increased the complexity of the search space, however, I started noticing that the behaviour of the algorithm was not always what I expected.

That raised more questions.

Was I measuring the right kind of entropy?

Is a feature with high categorical entropy necessarily useful when the actual action is a binary question?

How does feature cardinality affect the strategy?

What makes one question better than another?

Those questions eventually led to multiple agents and a benchmarking system, including:

Random Agent
Entropy Agent
Binary Information Gain Agent
Epsilon-Information Gain Agent
DQN Agent

The project gradually shifted from "Can I make an algorithm play Guess Who?" toward:

How do different decision-making strategies behave when the same search environment changes?

The current project is therefore both a game and an experimentation environment.

How the Game Works

The project has three main ways of interacting with the guessing environment.

Interactive Play

The game can be played through a user-facing mode where a human provides answers to the agent's questions.

This was part of the original idea: allowing the same guessing logic to interact with a real player rather than only a simulated target.

System Simulation

A system opponent can automatically select and hold a hidden character.

This allows the agents to play complete games without human interaction:

Agent selects action
        ↓
System checks hidden character
        ↓
System returns Yes / No
        ↓
Search space is updated
        ↓
Agent receives the new state
        ↓
Repeat

This makes repeated simulations and controlled experiments possible.

RL Environment

The project also includes a custom Gymnasium environment used by the DQN agent.

This separates the game mechanics from the learning algorithm and allows the environment to expose observations, process actions, calculate rewards, and manage episode termination.

