agents/

Contains the different decision-making strategies.

The agents share the same underlying guessing problem but approach it differently, making it possible to compare their behaviour under the same conditions.

The current implementations include:

a random baseline;
my original entropy-based strategy;
a binary information-gain strategy;
an epsilon-based variation;
a Reinforcement Learning agent.
analytics/

Contains the tools used to run simulations and analyze results.

Rather than judging an agent from a single game, the simulation system can run repeated episodes and collect metrics such as:

win rate;
number of steps;
search-space contraction;
and other performance trends.

The dashboard was built to make those results easier to observe instead of relying entirely on raw terminal output.

data/

Contains the character dataset and the dataset generation logic.

The generator allows the search environment to be created and expanded rather than treating the game as a fixed collection of characters.

This became important as I started experimenting with how changes in the search space affected agent behaviour.

environments/

Contains the game environments.

system_opponent.py

Handles the simulated opponent and the hidden target during automated games.

gym_env.py

Contains the custom Gymnasium environment used for Reinforcement Learning.

This environment handles the interaction loop between the RL agent and the guessing game.

Stores trained RL models.

The current project includes a trained DQN policy used by the RL agent.

main.py

The main entry point for running the project and accessing the available modes.

Current Focus: Comparing Decision Strategies

The goal is not simply to find the agent with the highest win rate.

I am interested in how and why the agents behave differently.

For example:

Does increasing the number of candidates change a strategy's effectiveness?
How does feature cardinality affect an entropy-based approach?
What happens when the action space becomes extremely large?
How much information does an RL agent need about the environment?
When does providing useful state information become too close to giving the agent the solution?
Can a learned policy generalize beyond the environments it was trained on?

These questions are what gradually pushed the project toward Reinforcement Learning experimentation.

The Guessing Game is still evolving, but I am deliberately treating the development process itself as part of the project.

Development Journey

One thing I want to preserve in this repository is how the project actually developed.

The final structure makes the path look more planned than it really was.

It wasn't.

I started with a simple idea for a guessing game, implemented a strategy based on how we humans might play, noticed behaviour I did not expect, and kept following the questions that came from those results.

That eventually led me into problems involving:

information theory;
binary search partitions;
large RL action spaces;
exploration failure;
action masking;
state representation;
potential information leakage;
and reward shaping.

Some of the most important parts of the project came from approaches that initially failed.

The detailed documentation for this journey will cover each stage more closely:

